1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,853
SUSIE:

2
00:00:02,853 --> 00:00:07,897
So Luke, for our next psychology assignment we have to do something on sleep

3
00:00:07,897 --> 00:00:08,580
and dreams.

4
00:00:08,580 --> 00:00:09,022
LUKE:

5
00:00:09,022 --> 00:00:09,404
Right.

6
00:00:09,404 --> 00:00:14,427
I've just read an article suggesting why we tend to forget most of our

7
00:00:14,427 --> 00:00:16,620
dreams soon after we wake up.

8
00:00:16,620 --> 00:00:20,202
I mean, most of my dreams aren't that interesting anyway,

9
00:00:20,202 --> 00:00:27,007
but what it said was that if we remembered everything, we might get mixed up about what actually happened

10
00:00:27,007 --> 00:00:28,440
and what we dreamed.

11
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:31,140
So it's a sort of protection.

12
00:00:31,140 --> 00:00:33,540
I hadn't heard that idea before.

13
00:00:33,540 --> 00:00:37,681
I'd always assumed that it was just that we didn't have room in our

14
00:00:37,681 --> 00:00:39,160
memories for all that stuff.

15
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:39,210
SUSIE:

16
00:00:39,210 --> 00:00:40,244
Me too.

17
00:00:40,244 --> 00:00:45,300
What do you think about the idea that our dreams may predict the future?

18
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:45,529
LUKE:

19
00:00:45,529 --> 00:00:47,820
It's a belief that you get all over the world.

20
00:00:47,820 --> 00:00:48,184
SUSIE:

21
00:00:48,184 --> 00:00:52,548
Yeah, lots of people have a story of it happening to them,

22
00:00:52,548 --> 00:00:57,639
but the explanation I've read is that for each dream that comes true, we

23
00:00:57,639 --> 00:00:59,093
have thousands that don't,

24
00:00:59,093 --> 00:01:02,730
but we don't notice those, we don't even remember them.

25
00:01:02,730 --> 00:01:08,099
We just remember the ones where something in the real world, like a view

26
00:01:08,099 --> 00:01:11,550
or an action, happens to trigger a dream memory.

27
00:01:11,550 --> 00:01:12,010
LUKE:

28
00:01:12,010 --> 00:01:12,470
Right.

29
00:01:12,470 --> 00:01:15,230
So it's just a coincidence really.

30
00:01:15,230 --> 00:01:20,340
Something else I read about is what they call segmented sleeping.

31
00:01:20,340 --> 00:01:26,070
That's a theory that hundreds of years ago, people used to get up in the middle of the night

32
00:01:26,070 --> 00:01:29,990
and have a chat or something to eat, then go back to bed.

33
00:01:29,990 --> 00:01:31,790
So I tried it myself.

34
00:01:31,790 --> 00:01:32,340
SUSIE:

35
00:01:32,340 --> 00:01:32,890
Why?

36
00:01:32,890 --> 00:01:33,172
LUKE:

37
00:01:33,172 --> 00:01:35,430
Well it's meant to make you more creative.

38
00:01:35,430 --> 00:01:36,627
I don't know why.

39
00:01:36,627 --> 00:01:39,020
But I gave it up after a week.

40
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:41,820
It just didn't fit in with my lifestyle.

41
00:01:41,820 --> 00:01:42,182
SUSIE:

42
00:01:42,182 --> 00:01:46,890
But most pre-school children have a short sleep in the day don't they?

43
00:01:46,890 --> 00:01:51,923
There was an experiment some students did here last term to see at what

44
00:01:51,923 --> 00:01:54,080
age kids should stop having naps.

45
00:01:54,080 --> 00:01:56,840
But they didn't really find an answer.

46
00:01:56,840 --> 00:02:01,073
They spent a lot of time working out the most appropriate methodology,

47
00:02:01,073 --> 00:02:04,600
but the results didn't seem to show any obvious patterns.

48
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:05,145
LUKE:

49
00:02:05,145 --> 00:02:05,690
Right.

50
00:02:05,690 --> 00:02:08,960
Anyway, let's think about our assignment.

51
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:14,127
Last time I had problems with the final stage, where we had to describe

52
00:02:14,127 --> 00:02:17,080
and justify how successful we thought we'd been.

53
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:20,150
I struggled a bit with the action plan too.

54
00:02:20,150 --> 00:02:20,484
SUSIE:

55
00:02:20,484 --> 00:02:22,489
I was OK with the planning,

56
00:02:22,489 --> 00:02:25,830
